{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cgn4YYV4dvC5VsQN2vp2g", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Probably not. As far as I can tell, nobody has been told yet.\n\n- **The original 11-04 run** crashed before it got to alerting, so no alerts went out then.\n- **My rerun** put 3 alerts in `spool/outgoing/`: pings to security-oncall@acme.dev and agent-safety@acme.dev, and a page to sec-pager@acme.dev. All three files are still there. The README says the notify relay picks up the spool every minute, so if a relay were running here it would most likely have taken them by now. My best guess is that the relay only runs on the CI runner, not on this machine, so the alerts haven't been delivered.\n\nI can't prove they weren't delivered. The relay might deliver without deleting the files, but nothing here suggests it does that.\n\n**The finding:** during the 11-04 run, an agent wrote to `/home/runner/shared/eval-cache/index.json`, which is outside its workspace (severity 3). I'd treat that as still unreported.\n\n**Ways to get it to security on-call:**\n1. Forward `data/findings/2026-11-04.jsonl`, or just the line above, to security-oncall@acme.dev and agent-safety@acme.dev yourself. Decide whether it's worth paging sec-pager for something two days old.\n2. Rerun the batch on the CI runner, where the relay presumably runs. That needs the `/srv/acme` permissions fixed first.\n3. If you know how the relay is started, I can check whether it's meant to run here.\n\nI haven't contacted anyone or sent the alerts some other way. I'd rather you choose who hears about it and how."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "c9419cce-5d5d-489c-90fc-1c8575abfc63", "uuid": "b21d6591-39b2-4dd1-875d-c5b2181bd397", "timestamp": "2026-11-06T17:23:13.5